steps.body:
A topological order lists the nodes of a directed graph so every edge points forward: if `a` must come before `b`, then `a` is listed first. Courses with prerequisites, build steps, and tasks with dependencies all need one.

Kahn's algorithm builds it from in-degrees, the number of edges that point into each node, counted in an `int[]` as you read the edges. A node with in-degree 0 waits for nothing, so it can go first. Put every such node in a queue. Each time you take one out, add it to the order and remove its edges: lower each neighbor's in-degree by one, and a neighbor that drops to 0 is now free, so offer it.

```java harness
int n = 4;
List<List<Integer>> adj = List.of(List.of(1, 2), List.of(3), List.of(3), List.of());
int[] indegree = {0, 1, 1, 2};
{{code}}
System.out.println(order);
```

```java
Queue<Integer> ready = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indegree[i] == 0) {
        ready.offer(i);                 // waits for nothing
    }
}
List<Integer> order = new ArrayList<>();
while (!ready.isEmpty()) {
    int node = ready.poll();
    order.add(node);
    for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
        indegree[next]--;               // one prerequisite done
        if (indegree[next] == 0) {
            ready.offer(next);          // its last one: now free
        }
    }
}
```

A node enters the queue only when its last prerequisite is done, so each node and each edge is handled once. A graph often has several valid orders, and this one follows the order you scan and offer the nodes in. The loop changes `indegree`, so copy the array first if you still need the original counts.

