steps.body:
Kahn's algorithm only takes a node out once all its prerequisites have come out. Nodes on a cycle wait on each other forever: their in-degrees never reach 0, so they never enter the queue. That gives you a cycle check for free. When the queue runs empty, compare the size of the order with `n`. If some nodes are missing, the graph has a cycle and no topological order exists:

```java harness
int n = 3;
List<Integer> order = new ArrayList<>(List.of(0));
int[] indegree = {0, 1, 1};
{{code}}
```

```java
if (order.size() == n) {
    System.out.println("Order: " + order);
} else {
    System.out.println("Cycle: " + (n - order.size()) + " nodes never came out");
}
```

This is the course schedule question, can you finish every course? The answer is yes exactly when `order.size() == n`.

The nodes that never came out still have an in-degree above 0 when the loop ends. They are the nodes on a cycle and every node that depends on one, so a loop over `indegree` after the search lists them. Put the search in a method that returns the order, and the caller can make the check.

