steps.body:
Union find, also called a disjoint set, keeps nodes in groups and answers one question fast: are these two in the same group? Each group is a tree stored in an `int[] parent`, and the tree's root names the group. At the start every node is its own root. Define the class after `Main`:

```java
class UnionFind {
    int[] parent;
    int[] size;

    UnionFind(int n) {
        parent = new int[n];
        size = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            parent[i] = i;                // every node starts alone
            size[i] = 1;
        }
    }

    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = find(parent[x]);  // path compression
        }
        return parent[x];
    }

    void union(int a, int b) {
        int ra = find(a);
        int rb = find(b);
        if (ra == rb) {
            return;                       // already one group
        }
        if (size[ra] < size[rb]) {        // the smaller tree goes under the bigger
            int t = ra;
            ra = rb;
            rb = t;
        }
        parent[rb] = ra;
        size[ra] += size[rb];
    }
}
UnionFind uf = new UnionFind(4);
uf.union(0, 1);
System.out.println(uf.find(1) == uf.find(0)); // true
```

`find` climbs the parents to the root, and **path compression** points every node it passes straight at that root, so the next `find` takes one step. `union` links one root under the other. **Union by size** hangs the smaller tree under the bigger one, so trees stay shallow, and `size[root]` is the group's size. Together they make each call close to constant time.

Two nodes share a group exactly when `find(a) == find(b)`. Compare roots, never `parent[a] == parent[b]`: a parent can be any node higher up the same tree.

