steps.body:
Give `union` two more jobs: return whether it joined two different groups, and keep a `count` of groups. The count starts at `n` in the constructor and drops by one for every real join:

```java harness
static int[] parent = {0, 1, 2};
static int[] size = {1, 1, 1};
static int count = 3;
static int find(int x) { if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]); return parent[x]; }
{{code}}
System.out.println(union(0, 1) + " " + union(1, 0) + " " + count);
```

```java
boolean union(int a, int b) {
    int ra = find(a);
    int rb = find(b);
    if (ra == rb) {
        return false;                 // already connected
    }
    if (size[ra] < size[rb]) {
        int t = ra;
        ra = rb;
        rb = t;
    }
    parent[rb] = ra;
    size[ra] += size[rb];
    count--;                          // two groups became one
    return true;
}
```

After every edge has gone through `union`, `count` is the number of connected components, with no DFS and no visited array.

The `false` answer means something too. In an undirected graph, an edge whose two ends are already connected closes a cycle, because a path between them existed before the edge. Every such edge is redundant: remove it and everything stays connected. To connect every group, you need `count - 1` more edges.

